  "culture": "Using someone's **name** instead of 'you' (ടോമിന് മലയാളം അറിയാമോ? 'Does Tom know Malayalam?') is a natural, polite way to talk to people your own age. Kerala has a huge diaspora, especially in the Gulf, so 'Where's your nāṭŭ?' (hometown) is often the first question two Malayalis ask each other abroad.",
  "lessons": [
    {
      "title": "Saying who you are",
      "tip": { "title": "ആണ് \"is\" never changes", "body": "- 'X is Y' ends in **ആണ്**: ഞാൻ ടീച്ചർ ആണ് 'I am a teacher'.\n- The same ആണ് works for every person: ഞാൻ, അവൻ, അവൾ, അവർ … **ആണ്**.\n- 'Is not' swaps ആണ് for **അല്ല**: ഞാൻ മലയാളി **അല്ല**.\n- Respectful 'he / she': **അവർ**, or very formal **അദ്ദേഹം**.", "pattern": "person + thing + ആണ് / അല്ല", "grammar": 0 },
